You are a kask skill validator. Validate the scaffolded crate against all check categories.

## Scaffolded Crate

```json
{{ scaffolded_crate | tojson }}
```

## Check Catalog

The checks below mirror `skill-maintenance-validate.j2` (R1-R12, Z1-Z8,
X1-X4, E1-E16) exactly — this template delegates to it and only produces
fix suggestions. Keep the two enumerations in sync: same ID, same meaning.
Do not add create-skill-only checks under shared IDs; if a check is missing
from the canonical validator, promote it there in a separate change.

## Step 1 — Registry Checks (R1-R12)

Validate:
- R1: manifest.yaml exists — FlowDef manifest at `registry/manifests/<name>.yaml` AND crate manifest at `registry/templates/<name>/manifest.yaml`.
- R2: Crate metadata valid — `crate.name`, `crate.version`, `crate.description` all present.
- R3: Templates list non-empty — at least one entry in the `templates` array.
- R4: Template entry valid — each entry has `id`, `path`, `type`, `description`.
- R5: template_type valid — each .j2 `template_type` in {WordAct, KnowAct, FlowDef}.
- R6: Contract valid — each .j2 has `contract.input` and `contract.output` with typed fields.
- R8: Visibility valid — each .j2 `visibility` in {Private, Public, Shared}.
- R9: .j2 file exists — each template entry's `path` resolves to an actual .j2 file.
- R10: [inference] frontmatter valid — each .j2 starts with an `[inference]` block.
- R11: template_type valid — all templates declare a valid `template_type` (KnowAct, WordAct, FlowDef, RenderAct).
- R12: Jinja2 body present — each .j2 has a template body after the `---` separator.

## Step 2 — Companion Checks (Z1-Z8)

Validate:
- Z1: SKILL.md exists (informational) — file present in `.agents/skills/<name>/`.
- Z2: Frontmatter valid — `---` delimiters with `name` and `description`.
- Z3: Name matches directory — `name` field == directory basename, lowercase-hyphenated.
- Z4: Description present — non-empty, 1–1024 chars.
- Z5: No Magna Carta violations — no instructions bypassing user sovereignty or consent.
- Z6: Headless compliance — no visual UI, Grafana, dashboards.
- Z7: No deprecated markers — no `todo!`, `unimplemented!`, `#[deprecated]`.
- Z8: Derived from registry — SKILL.md methodology aligns with registry templates.

## Step 3 — Cross-Artifact Checks (X1-X4)

Validate:
- X1: Registry exists — manifest.yaml present (Critical).
- X2: No orphan SKILL.md — SKILL.md without a registry crate is incomplete (High).
- X3: No drift — SKILL.md does not claim behaviors the registry templates do not support (Medium).
- X4: Bidirectional name match — every `.agents/skills/<name>/` has a matching `registry/manifests/<name>.yaml`, and vice versa. Report exact mismatches by name (High).

## Step 4 — Executor Compliance (E1-E16)

These checks mirror `skill-maintenance-validate.j2` E1-E16 exactly (this
template delegates to it). Keep the two enumerations in sync — same ID,
same meaning — or fix suggestions will reference the wrong check.

Validate:
- E1: Action compliance — every step's `action` is canonical (select, populate, compute, execute, feedback, validate, retrieve, render, flowdef, loop, choice, abort, escalate). No non-canonical actions.
- E2: rJoule block present — `rjoule:` block; `rjoule.cap` > 0 if any step uses `action: select` (inference).
- E3: Convergence block — for `category: skill`: `convergence:` with `convergence_mode` (e.g. "cauchy", "" for single-pass), `max_iterations`, `min_iterations`, `on_not_reached` (abort or escalate). Cauchy-mode manifests should have `cauchy_epsilon` and `target_artifacts_field`.
- E4: Category valid — `manifest.category` is one of: skill, qa-script, runtime-config, daemon-process, pipeline.
- E5: Template ref resolvable — every `template_ref` resolves to an embedded `.j2` or `.yaml` in `registry/templates/`.
- E8: Sub-manifest budgets — any `template_ref` to a `.yaml` sub-manifest (FlowDef recursion) has `rjoule` block.
- E9: Step description — every step has a non-empty `description` field.
- E10: Ordinal sequence — step ordinals are sequential starting from 1 (or 0 for pre-processing steps).
- E11: Ledger span namespace — `ledger.span_namespace` present and equals `reg.skill.<manifest.id>`; no abolished `spans:` list.
- E12: lisp.eval form present — every `compute` step with `compute_ref: lisp.eval` has a non-empty `form` string in `input_mapping`.
- E13: lisp.eval step_N_result references backward — every `{{ step_N_result }}` in a `lisp.eval` step's `env` references an ordinal strictly less than the current step's ordinal.
- E14: lisp.eval form syntax dry-run — the `form` string is valid Lisp syntax per the `hkask_lisp` interpreter.
- E15: on_failure config on execute steps — every `action: execute` step should have `on_failure` with `action: report` and non-empty `resume` text.
- E16: execute step mcp field references known tool — every `action: execute` step's `mcp:` field references a tool in the known MCP tool list.

## Step 5 — Produce Fix Suggestions

For any failures, produce actionable fix suggestions with:
- check: which check failed
- severity: critical/high/medium/low
- issue: what's wrong
- fix: how to fix it (specific file, line, change)

## Output

Produce a JSON object with: validation_results (per-check pass/fail with evidence), pass_count, fail_count, executor_compliance ({pass_count, fail_count}), fix_suggestions (array), next_research_focus (one-sentence directive for the next iteration's research phase — e.g. 'find better ontological anchors for the convergence model' or '' if validation passed).
